WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.469
Film Courage: Is it important to know the
ending before you start writing your screenplays?

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:08.800
Van Ditthavong, Filmmaker/Photographer: I
mean I would love to know the ending all of

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:09.800
the time.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.800
It will be awesome.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.250
Sometimes I have it and sometimes it changes.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:20.320
I would love to know as many parts of the
puzzle as I can.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.150
Sometimes you discover it and it goes another
way.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:30.010
I think that’s the beauty of sitting down
and chipping things away because it might

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.260
make more sense.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:37.710
A lot of times it anchors things and having
an ending where you are concrete about it

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:43.120
helps.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.149
But then you might fall in love with the characters
and you don’t want it to end that way, so

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:55.010
again trying to be flexible with that is good
in my opinion but knowing the ending would

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:03.980
be awesome and sometimes I have that in my
head and then you play around with the themes

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:09.649
and then you follow the themes and hopefully
you can direct everything toward the end.

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:11.210
That would be my process.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.409
I don’t know what works all the time.

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:18.079
Film Courage: But as a rule it sounds like
you don’t always have to know the ending

00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:19.740
before you begin something?

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.869
Van: Yes, I think so.

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:22.869
I think so.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:32.460
It would be very helpful and it will just
guide but I think you have a gist of your

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:39.959
story and then if you gist then you probably
can get to the ending quicker than if you

00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:40.959
don’t have that.

00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:47.149
Film Courage: With ALL ROADS TO PEARLA did
you know the ending as you began?

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:50.960
Van: I had an idea of an ending.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:57.729
Now knowing how to tie it all up it took me
awhile.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:06.630
I remember I did have it where I knew how
I wanted it to end but how it ended was still

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.610
up in the air because there were so many loose
parts.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:17.709
I think one of the things that I’ve found
even with the script in the early stages is

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:23.819
I don’t know if anyone out there has a similar
problem is your storylines, sometimes you’ve

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:33.769
got to wrangle them back and have that fit
and that was the tricky part and not make

00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:40.489
it seem so forced and things and massage it
correctly and still get your message across

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:43.790
or what the story is about.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:52.959
When I started I had an idea what’s going
to happen but without giving anything away

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:57.579
it was guided but the little details was harder.

00:02:57.579 --> 00:03:02.390
Film Courage: Maybe help me understand that
so is it almost like if you’re going to

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:07.430
search for Halloween decorations but then
you go down this rabbit hole and now you are

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:12.959
searching fall [decorations]…and you go
“Wait, my original plan was to look for

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.290
Halloween decorations.”

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.400
Is that what you’re talking about where
you go…where you have to wrangle it back

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.400
in?

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:30.329
Van: Yes, like say for instance we had skeletons,
we had zombies, and we have ghosts but only

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:34.849
the ghosts is at the end.

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:42.189
What happened to the skeleton and the zombies
without it becoming…did we forget them at

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:47.239
the aisle 14 or did they disappear?

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:49.010
Or did the ghost take it?

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:59.480
So if you knew that the ghost is the one that’s
there at the end in the front yard because

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.560
then maybe you won’t want the zombies in
the back later.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.480
You know you have the elements but you know
you thought you wanted the ghosts to be the

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.950
one that is the main highlight.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:12.549
I think that is what I’m talking about.

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:17.919
More of like I have an idea I want the ghost
to be my the guy out there but what do I do

00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:20.470
with the rest of the crew.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:26.009
Film Courage: Interesting, okay…and do we
really need to know how much and why you’ve

00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:30.020
left the skeleton in the aisle 14 or 14 [whatever]?

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:35.740
Van: That goes back to our discussion of do
you want to hand hold more or do you want

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:44.670
to leave it a little bit more obscure?

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:51.930
So do we leave a foot out and we see the skeleton
foot in aisle 14 or do you want the entire

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:57.400
leg out or do you want the entire body out
so you can see that the skeleton was hurt

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.930
in aisle 14?

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:04.259
That kind of stuff so that’s what I think
I’m talking about in terms of like have

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:10.479
an ending, I have a feeling ending, how do
I get there with all of these other things

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:11.650
that are happening?

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:17.810
But then sometimes you go you know what…actually
the skeleton should be the one that’s at

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:18.949
the end.

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:20.360
So that could happen.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:29.220
But with ALL ROADS TO PEARLA I had a feeling
of what I wanted, my challenge was I knew

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.400
the last page.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:37.380
My challenge was the 5 pages prior to that
last page.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:44.250
That kind of thing) of tying things up and
getting it right and who is and how do we

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:50.661
get there to make it kind of dramatic and
suspenseful (things like that).

00:05:50.661 --> 00:05:51.661
Question For The Viewers:

00:05:51.661 --> 00:05:51.673
Will you start writing a screenplay if you
don’t know the ending?

